/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132364 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132364 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1097861c-08b5-51d1-b144-b694e210e2ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132365 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 25 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 32 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 42 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 15 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132365 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132365 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5aceca29-5683-5168-8c82-fefc734e9525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132366 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132366 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7583ded6-1034-5636-a7db-44cdd44a6017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133617 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133617 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$7$** | **$6.5$** | **$6$** | **$5$** | **$3$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n![](images/9d/9da2a49a4bab645fa1b8ea3f98e6aa6135b191e121d0cc78a73b517fbc510fc0.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133617 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18e10da5-f3e1-52d2-b2c6-2a69a1bf1947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133617 #edb/ref 1074 "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}7$** | **$\\color{purple}6.5$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}5$** | **$\\color{purple}3$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}5$ | $\\color{red}9$ | $\\color{red}15$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have\n![](images/b5/b5302251a49a45514a55d2987289548e2a55917884ff4c240be081ba512a970e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186133618 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133618 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$-8$** | **$-2$** | **$6$** | **$15$** |\n| $x_3$ | **$8$** | **$6$** | **$4$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $20$ | $40$ | $60$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n![](images/3f/3f86f03e3a32ede2ac18d6c39f62de5686a74d4050b8c1d7aa2a6fc3e2cdddbb.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133618 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab93579a-f7a1-5cfe-85ce-5a4d5722e7b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133618 #edb/ref 1074 "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}-8$** | **$\\color{purple}-2$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}15$** |\n| $x_3$ | **$\\color{green}8$** | **$\\color{green}6$** | **$\\color{green}4$** | **$\\color{green}2$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}10$ | $\\color{red}20$ | $\\color{red}40$ | $\\color{red}60$ |\n\n \n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny= \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 60\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have \n\n![](images/c8/c83d300d9e6e1c1bd61fc49c4e849ab373d902beb3cd12ef501461a86bd1a08c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133808 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression That Passes Through the Origin" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133809 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5eaee4e9-7be5-5c35-bc88-89697a15d75a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134031 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134031 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134031 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134031 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134031 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134031 #edb/ref 1045 "Multiple Linear Regression With Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134031 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134031 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134031 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bff21f91-25dc-5812-8a0d-35d914692a82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134387 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134387 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134387 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y = β_{1}x_{1} + β_{2}x_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{1} \\\\ β_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y$,\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134387 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32203128-d23c-5cb0-ab0f-cdb995f482b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134709 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134709 #edb/ref 1061 20314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134709 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "958cc7d8-671b-5ebd-9b9d-c938658e82bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134709 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134710 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134710 #edb/ref 1061 20315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134710 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b6349ce-1de8-5cd8-aed5-641cee35b3ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134710 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134711 #edb/ref 1061 20317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134711 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2aaa8ea5-3420-58b2-86f9-e413b60cb5d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134711 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134891 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134891 #edb/ref 1061 37336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134891 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4adafa4a-1969-530d-9be3-b8157c980149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134891 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135064 #edb/ref 1064 "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2} &=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2} &=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2} &=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2} &=24\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186135064 #edb/ref 1065 3655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135064 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ac48d158-e2b0-57f9-b8ba-6fe587ca807a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135064 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135145 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186135145 #edb/ref 1065 6153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135145 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4e7ff642-aa4a-5099-ac22-42297dcfcacf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135145 #edb/ref 1067 "Regression Planes Passing Through the Origin" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270197 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -11 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12 \\\\ 5 & 20\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186270197 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270197 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "68c5ca04-5276-58b2-919f-db4547f0187c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270537 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 17 \\\\ 4 & 20 \\\\ 5 & 30\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186270537 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270537 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "91b75b9b-dd91-5aa0-9f17-6376114b5c3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270877 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186270877 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270877 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b4c0a5e3-4600-5755-a686-5f69ee54c619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271323 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 17 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 30\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186271323 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271323 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ea77b106-5638-57e6-a100-0131ccad7266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271363 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186271363 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271363 #ed